\title{Cybernetic Symbiosis: Sharp Convergence Bounds for Mutual Adaptive
Feedback in Human--Machine Interfaces}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 24, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop, from first principles, a complete and sharp convergence theory for
the \emph{co-adaptation} of a biological signal and a synthetic decoder in a
brain--computer interface (BCI). Modeling the human motor-cortex signal and the
decoder output as two scalar sequences that mutually adapt toward one another
with constant gains $a$ (human) and $b$ (decoder), we prove that the disagreement
between the two channels obeys the exact linear recursion
$e_{n+1} = (1 - a - b)e_n$, hence follows the closed geometric envelope
$|e_n| = |1 - a - b|^n |h_0 - d_0|$. This identity yields a complete
classification of the loop's asymptotic behavior in terms of the total gain
$s = a + b$: the loop converges to consensus if and only if $0 < s < 2$; it
converges instantly (in a single step) at the critical value $s = 1$; and it
diverges when $s$ leaves $[0,2]$. We exhibit a conserved quantity
$b\,h_n + a\,d_n$, from which both channels are shown to converge to the
gain-weighted average $(b\,h_0 + a\,d_0)/(a+b)$ of their initial states. Finally
we give an explicit counterexample refuting the naive conjecture that mutual
adaptation always yields agreement: at maximal gains $a = b = 1$ the loop
oscillates with constant amplitude forever.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

A brain--computer interface couples a living nervous system to a decoding
algorithm. In practice both ends are \emph{plastic}: the user learns to modulate
neural activity to achieve a desired effect, while the decoder is retrained to
better interpret the user's signals. This creates a two-sided adaptive feedback
loop---a \emph{co-adaptation}---whose stability is not obvious a priori. If both
parties chase a moving target that is itself the other party, does the
interaction settle into agreement, or can it destabilize?

This paper answers the question completely for the canonical scalar model of
mutual linear adaptation. Our contributions are: an exact error law
(Section~\ref{sec:error}); a sharp convergence criterion (Section~\ref{sec:conv});
a conservation law and consensus value (Section~\ref{sec:cons}); a critical-damping
optimum (Section~\ref{sec:crit}); and an instability analysis with an explicit
counterexample (Section~\ref{sec:inst}).

\section{The model}

We work over the real numbers throughout.

\begin{definition}[Co-adaptation dynamics]
Fix human gain $a \in \mathbb{R}$, decoder gain $b \in \mathbb{R}$, and an initial
joint state $p_0 = (h_0, d_0) \in \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}$. The joint state
after $n$ rounds is $\mathrm{state}(n) = (h_n, d_n)$ defined by
\[
  \mathrm{state}(0) = (h_0, d_0), \qquad
  \mathrm{state}(n+1) = \bigl((1-a)h_n + a\,d_n,\ (1-b)d_n + b\,h_n\bigr).
\]
We call $h_n$ the human channel, $d_n$ the decoder channel, and define the
disagreement $e_n = h_n - d_n$.
\end{definition}

At each round the human moves a fraction $a$ of the way toward the decoder's
output, and the decoder moves a fraction $b$ toward the human's signal. We set the
\emph{contraction factor} $q = 1 - a - b$ and the \emph{total gain} $s = a + b$,
so $q = 1 - s$.

\section{The exact error law}\label{sec:error}

\begin{theorem}[Error recursion]\label{thm:step}
For all $n$, $\; e_{n+1} = (1 - a - b)\,e_n.$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By definition,
\[
  e_{n+1} = \bigl[(1-a)h_n + a\,d_n\bigr] - \bigl[(1-b)d_n + b\,h_n\bigr]
          = (1-a-b)h_n - (1-a-b)d_n = (1-a-b)e_n. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form]\label{thm:closed}
For all $n$, $\; e_n = (1 - a - b)^n\,e_0.$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$: the base case $e_0 = q^0 e_0$ is immediate, and
$e_{n+1} = q\,e_n = q \cdot q^n e_0 = q^{n+1} e_0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:step}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Geometric envelope]\label{cor:env}
For all $n$, $\; |e_n| = |1 - a - b|^n\,|h_0 - d_0|.$
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[One-step contraction]
For all $n$, $\; |e_{n+1}| = |1 - a - b|\cdot|e_n|.$
\end{corollary}

The disagreement rides an exact geometric trajectory, multiplied by $|q|$ each
round. The long-term behavior depends only on $|q| = |1-a-b|$, hence only on the
total gain $s$; the split of effort between the parties is irrelevant.

\section{Convergence}\label{sec:conv}

\begin{theorem}[Convergence to agreement]\label{thm:conv}
If $|1 - a - b| < 1$, then $e_n \to 0$ as $n \to \infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}, $e_n = q^n e_0$ with $|q| < 1$, so $q^n \to 0$ and
$e_n \to 0$.
\end{proof}

The condition $|1-a-b| < 1$ is equivalent to $0 < a + b < 2$: the total gain must
lie in the open stability window $(0,2)$.

\section{Conservation law and consensus}\label{sec:cons}

\begin{theorem}[Conservation law]\label{thm:inv}
The gain-weighted combination is invariant:
$b\,h_n + a\,d_n = b\,h_0 + a\,d_0$ for all $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$. For the step,
$b\,h_{n+1} + a\,d_{n+1}
 = b[(1-a)h_n + a d_n] + a[(1-b)d_n + b h_n]
 = b\,h_n + a\,d_n$,
since the cross terms recombine, and the result follows by the inductive
hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Closed forms of the channels]\label{prop:chan}
If $a + b \neq 0$, then for all $n$,
\[
  h_n = \frac{b\,h_0 + a\,d_0 + a\,e_n}{a + b}, \qquad
  d_n = \frac{b\,h_0 + a\,d_0 - b\,e_n}{a + b}.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Clear denominators and substitute $e_n = h_n - d_n$ into
Theorem~\ref{thm:inv}; both are then linear identities.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Consensus]\label{thm:consensus}
If $a + b \neq 0$ and $|1 - a - b| < 1$, then
\[
  \lim_{n\to\infty} h_n = \lim_{n\to\infty} d_n = \frac{b\,h_0 + a\,d_0}{a + b}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:conv}, $e_n \to 0$; passing to the limit in
Proposition~\ref{prop:chan} kills the $e_n$-terms.
\end{proof}

The consensus is the gain-weighted average of the initial states, with
cross-coupled weights: the party that adapts less aggressively exerts more
influence on the compromise.

\section{Critical damping}\label{sec:crit}

\begin{theorem}[Critical damping]\label{thm:crit}
If $a + b = 1$, then $e_{n+1} = 0$ for every $n$; in particular $e_1 = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Here $q = 1 - a - b = 0$, so $e_{n+1} = q\,e_n = 0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:step}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Immediate agreement]
If $a + b = 1$, then $h_{n+1} = d_{n+1}$ for all $n$: the channels agree from
round one, regardless of the initial gap.
\end{corollary}

At the critical total gain the disagreement is annihilated in a single step---the
fastest achievable rate and the natural design target for a co-adaptive interface.

\section{Instability and the failure of naive convergence}\label{sec:inst}

\begin{theorem}[Contrarian instability]\label{thm:div}
If $|1 - a - b| > 1$ and $h_0 \neq d_0$, then $|e_n| \to \infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:env}, $|e_n| = |q|^n |h_0 - d_0|$ with $|q| > 1$ and
$|h_0 - d_0| > 0$, so $|e_n| \to \infty$.
\end{proof}

This establishes necessity: convergence for all initial gaps holds if and only if
$0 < a + b < 2$.

\begin{theorem}[Perpetual oscillation]\label{thm:osc}
With $a = b = 1$ and $(h_0, d_0) = (1, 0)$, one has $|e_n| = 1$ for all $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Here $q = -1$ and $e_0 = 1$, so $e_n = (-1)^n$ and $|e_n| = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Refutation of naive convergence]
The claim ``every mutual adaptive feedback loop reaches agreement'' is false: at
$a = b = 1$ the sequence $e_n = (-1)^n$ does not converge to $0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
If $e_n \to 0$ then $|e_n| \to 0$; but $|e_n| = 1 \to 1$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:osc}, contradicting uniqueness of limits.
\end{proof}

\section{The stability trichotomy}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}
\toprule
Regime & Total gain $s=a+b$ & $|q|$ & Behavior \\
\midrule
Convergent & $0 < s < 2$ & $<1$ & $e_n \to 0$; consensus at $(bh_0+ad_0)/(a+b)$ \\
Critically damped & $s = 1$ & $0$ & $e_n = 0$ for $n \ge 1$ \\
Marginal & $s = 0$ or $s = 2$ & $=1$ & constant amplitude; oscillation \\
Divergent & $s < 0$ or $s > 2$ & $>1$ & $|e_n| \to \infty$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The phases are harmony, stalemate, and chaos, separated by the sharp thresholds
$s = 0, 1, 2$.

\section{Applications and future directions}

Although framed around brain--computer interfaces, the model applies to any pair
of adaptive agents in mutual linear feedback: adaptive BCIs (keep total gain in
$(0,2)$, tune toward $1$), multi-agent consensus and control, and algorithmic
negotiation. A key conceptual point is that symmetry of intent does not guarantee
convergence---stability is a property of the total gain, not of good faith.

Natural extensions include: vector/multichannel interfaces with matrix gains
$A, B$, where the criterion becomes $\rho(I - A - B) < 1$ on the spectral radius;
time-varying and stochastic gains (convergence under
$\prod_n |1 - a_n - b_n| \to 0$, or almost-sure convergence with noise); nonlinear
decoders via Banach fixed-point theory; an optimal-gain/regret formulation of
critical damping; and packaging the trichotomy as a control-theoretic phase
diagram.

\section{Conclusion}

From a two-line update rule we obtained a complete convergence theory for
human--machine co-adaptation: the disagreement contracts by $q = 1 - a - b$ each
round; the loop converges iff $0 < a + b < 2$; it converges instantly at
$a + b = 1$; both channels meet at the gain-weighted average of their starts; and
it diverges---or oscillates forever at the boundary---otherwise. For reliable, fast
symbiosis, control the total adaptation effort and aim for the critical value one.

\end{document}

